WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.978
Each skill uses just one demonstration video,

2
00:00:07.978 --> 00:00:10.360
despite noise, viewpoint changes and occlusions.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.041
The robot works in a different scene,

4
00:00:13.041 --> 00:00:15.716
with its own body and new object locations.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.098
How can it preserve the interaction

6
00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:20.896
and connect these demonstrations into a complete task?

7
00:00:21.176 --> 00:00:25.424
We recover object motion from human video and prepare reusable skills.

8
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.946
Each skill retains the interaction that matters:

9
00:00:27.946 --> 00:00:30.898
how an object is held, where contact is maintained,

10
00:00:30.898 --> 00:00:32.538
and how the action ends.

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.088
The interaction stays reusable while robot motion adapts to the current scene.

12
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.114
Object roles locate the targets,

13
00:00:41.114 --> 00:00:44.200
and coordinated base and arm motion realises the task.

14
00:00:44.442 --> 00:00:47.010
New observations can revise the remaining motion.

15
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.431
These skills can be selected, ordered,

16
00:00:52.431 --> 00:00:55.338
and bound to objects in a shared workspace.

17
00:00:56.772 --> 00:00:59.251
Can disposal, tape placement, and wiping

18
00:00:59.251 --> 00:01:02.556
then become stages of one connected mobile task.

19
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.672
Separate demonstrations can form one continuous task.

20
00:01:13.568 --> 00:01:18.056
Skill boundaries retain scene context as the robot moves between objects.

21
00:01:18.241 --> 00:01:22.105
Each stage continues from the preceding robot configuration.

22
00:01:24.472 --> 00:01:28.312
Different interaction requirements are handled within the same sequence.

23
00:01:29.145 --> 00:01:32.745
Retained scene estimates provide the context for the next action.

24
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.737
Long-horizon tasks connect object interactions across locations.

25
00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:40.735
Loading establishes the conditions for transport and pouring,

26
00:01:40.735 --> 00:01:44.232
linking several skills into one continuous execution.

27
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.140
One arm keeps hold of the basket

28
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.011
while the other collects objects.

29
00:01:50.011 --> 00:01:53.004
The robot carries the growing collection between tables,

30
00:01:53.004 --> 00:01:55.624
reusing the same interaction at successive locations.

31
00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.162
Some tasks require overlapping roles.

32
00:02:06.654 --> 00:02:09.536
Bimanual coordination lets one arm maintain support

33
00:02:09.536 --> 00:02:12.006
while the other manipulates an object.

34
00:02:14.082 --> 00:02:15.262
Contact roles overlap,

35
00:02:15.262 --> 00:02:19.194
and support persists until the other arm has safely withdrawn.

36
00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:26.577
Wiping requires contact to persist as the tool moves across the surface.

37
00:02:29.751 --> 00:02:31.767
The transferred skill preserves tool alignment

38
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:34.791
while the robot adapts its motion to the scene.

39
00:02:43.294 --> 00:02:47.787
Door opening couples engagement with the handle to the motion of the door,

40
00:02:47.787 --> 00:02:50.206
extending the interaction beyond the initial grasp.

41
00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:56.849
SAKI connects reusable human interactions with scene-dependent robot motion,

42
00:02:56.849 --> 00:02:59.138
enabling longer mobile manipulation tasks.
